{
  "summary": "Adversarial triage of measured research claims: an isolated reader grounds each claim in its primary artifacts (H3); fresh independent skeptics (one confound each) reason over the committed numbers and emit a raw status + crux per confound (H1; H2 number-gates: falsifiability, margin, scope); unresolved cruxes become a run-queue (existing controls) or author-queue (controls to write); an optional read-only runner executes the run-queue. Queue items shared across claims are hoisted to a shared_queue so instrument-level checks run once, not per-claim (H4). No rolled-up score or verdict (the human reads the cruxes).",
  "agentCount": 72,
  "logs": [
    "Triaging 6 claim(s); confounds=10; execute_runs=true; ground=true."
  ],
  "result": {
    "claims": [
      {
        "id": "anchor-talktuner-demographics",
        "grounding": {
          "grounding": "reproduces",
          "note": "All four anchor accuracies, band arithmetic, test-family disjointness (f09-f11 only), row labels vs dataset.jsonl, and the three instrument sha256s reproduce exactly; gender OUT_OF_BAND confirmed independently from raw fp16 activations; education PERM_ANOMALY present as stated.",
          "observed": "Re-derived from per-item rows: age 0.9467 (244 rows; claim 0.9467), education 0.9399 (claim 0.9399), ses 1.0000 (claim 1.0000), gender 0.4467 = 67/150 (claim 0.4467); poscontrol topic 1.0000. My own numpy logistic probe on acts_anchor_gender.npz layer 6: val 0.98 / test 0.48 vs their 0.980 / 0.4467 (layer 27 would have given test 0.92). probe_summary_ALL.json anchor_in_band=false.",
          "artifacts_read": [
            "/home/hal/dev/interp/dm_hack/mats_open/auto_interp/PREREG.md",
            "/home/hal/dev/interp/dm_hack/mats_open/auto_interp/dataset.jsonl",
            "/home/hal/dev/interp/dm_hack/mats_open/auto_interp/dataset_manifest.json",
            "/home/hal/dev/interp/dm_hack/mats_open/auto_interp/out/convgen_selftest.txt",
            "/home/hal/dev/interp/dm_hack/mats_open/auto_interp/out/results_probe/out/probe_summary_anchor_age.json",
            "/home/hal/dev/interp/dm_hack/mats_open/auto_interp/out/results_probe/out/probe_summary_anchor_gender.json",
            "/home/hal/dev/interp/dm_hack/mats_open/auto_interp/out/results_probe/out/probe_summary_anchor_education.json",
            "/home/hal/dev/interp/dm_hack/mats_open/auto_interp/out/results_probe/out/probe_summary_anchor_ses.json",
            "/home/hal/dev/interp/dm_hack/mats_open/auto_interp/out/results_probe/out/probe_summary_poscontrol_topic.json",
            "/home/hal/dev/interp/dm_hack/mats_open/auto_interp/out/results_probe/out/probe_summary_ALL.json",
            "/home/hal/dev/interp/dm_hack/mats_open/auto_interp/out/results_probe/out/acts_anchor_gender.npz"
          ]
        },
        "findings": [
          {
            "confound": "off-distribution ablation",
            "status": "RULED_OUT",
            "crux": "The protocol contains no ablation step at all — it is a linear-probe readout (last-token residual after an appended fixed prompt, logistic regression per layer), with layer selection on val families and a single report on template-disjoint test families (dataset_manifest.json). \"Off-distribution ablation\" as a mechanism (activations pushed off-distribution by an intervention/ablation, inflating apparent accuracy) has no foothold here since nothing is ablated; the only distribution-shift risk in this design is train/val/test template leakage, which the manifest's 7/2/3 disjoint-family split and the passed permutation gates for the IN_BAND anchors (age 0.189/0.197, ses 0.383/0.202, both well under the |mean-chance|<=0.104 committed threshold) are built to catch and did not flag."
          },
          {
            "confound": "Downstream compensation / self-repair is a phenomenon observed under causal intervention: when a component (head, layer, direction) is ablated or patched, later layers partially restore the lost information/behavior, which can make accuracy or effect-size measurements look smaller than the \"true\" causal contribution of the ablated part. It requires an intervention step whose effect is measured at a later point.",
            "status": "RULED_OUT",
            "crux": "The protocol described (run_probe_study.py) is pure observational linear probing on frozen fp16 residual activations at the last token of an appended prompt, across all 41 layers, with per-layer logistic regression chosen on val and reported once on test — no ablation, patching, steering, or any other intervention on the network's forward pass appears anywhere in the described method or artifacts (dataset.jsonl, acts_{task}.npz, probe_summary_*.json). Self-repair requires a perturbation for downstream layers to compensate for; there is none here."
          },
          {
            "confound": "selection bias — the study could produce a spurious \"reproduces the band\" claim if the test items/layer were selected in a way that favors success (favorable subset, layer chosen on the same data it's tested on, or discarding anomalies)",
            "status": "RULED_OUT",
            "crux": "Layer selection happens on val cue-families and is reported once on a template-disjoint test split, with a prestated permutation gate — and both failure modes selection bias would predict (val/test divergence for gender; inflated permutation accuracy for education) were caught and reported as disqualifying rather than hidden, yielding the committed anchor_in_band=false rather than a falsely-inflated positive."
          },
          {
            "confound": "readout artifact: the TalkTuner-faithful readout (last-token residual of one fixed appended prompt \"I think the {attribute} of this user is\") may pick up superficial/template-bound cues correlated with the label in the 7 train + 2 val cue families rather than a genuine, generalizing attribute direction, so accuracy on the template-disjoint 3 test families collapses.",
            "status": "EXPLAINS",
            "crux": "anchor_gender's val accuracy (0.980, same layer 6) versus test accuracy (0.4467, below the 0.50 chance floor) is exactly the signature of a readout that fit surface/template features present in train+val families and failed to transfer to the disjoint test families — i.e., the probe was reading an artifact of phrasing/family structure captured by this fixed single-prompt last-token readout, not a genuine gender direction. recheck_rows.py MATCH and retrain_probe.sh REPRODUCES confirm the number is computed and reproduced correctly (not a bug), and the poscontrol topic probe (1.0000, clean perm) shows the pipeline/readout *can* extract a real signal when one exists — but neither control shows the gender readout itself is measuring the target attribute rather than family-specific surface structure, so the collapse remains consistent with (not ruled out from) a readout artifact rather than genuine non-encoding of gender. Because anchor_gender being OUT_OF_BAND is exactly what drives anchor_in_band=false, this confound plausibly produces the claim's headline (non-)result."
          },
          {
            "confound": "Regime specificity: probes may learn features tied to the narrow synthetic/template regime (indirect-cue conversation templates) rather than a generalizable demographic direction, producing accuracy that looks good in-regime but fails to transfer even within the same task/model.",
            "status": "EXPLAINS",
            "crux": "anchor_gender: val accuracy 0.980 vs test accuracy 0.4467 (below chance 0.50) at the identical chosen layer (6), where val and test are drawn from disjoint cue-template families by the preregistered 7/2/3 split — the probe generalizes within its training regime's template families but collapses on unseen template families, the defining signature of regime specificity, and this single collapse is what makes anchor_in_band=false for the whole claim."
          },
          {
            "confound": "construct validity — whether the \"indirect cue\" probe target actually captures the underlying demographic attribute (gender) as a stable, transferable construct, versus latching onto surface features specific to the cue-family templates used to elicit it",
            "status": "EXPLAINS",
            "crux": "anchor_gender: val accuracy 0.980 at layer 6 collapses to test accuracy 0.4467 (below chance 0.50) on the template-disjoint test cue families, with test-set permutation baselines at 0.493/0.527 — i.e., whatever the probe learned from train/val cue families carried no signal into unseen cue families. That is the signature of a probe fitting cue-family-specific correlates rather than a genuine, generalizable representation of \"gender,\" which is exactly what a construct-validity confound predicts, and no committed control addresses this: recheck_rows.py only re-verifies arithmetic (MATCH) and retrain_probe.sh only confirms the same layer/activations reproduce the same numbers (REPRODUCES) — neither tests whether the test-family cues instantiate the same construct as train/val families. The anchor_education PERM_ANOMALY (permuted-label test accuracies 0.546/0.410 far above chance 0.333) independently shows family-identity leakage into probe accuracy, reinforcing that \"accuracy\" on these splits partly reflects template/family structure rather than the demographic construct itself."
          },
          {
            "confound": "Scale incomparability would mean the published TalkTuner accuracies and this replication's accuracies aren't measured on comparable scales (different chance levels, different test-set composition/size, different accuracy definitions across tasks), so a flat published-minus-0.12 band is not a fair yardstick and apparent IN_BAND/OUT_OF_BAND calls could be artifacts of that scale mismatch rather than real reproduction success/failure.",
            "status": "RULED_OUT",
            "crux": "For anchor_gender the same probe, same layer (6), same accuracy scale and same n-convention scores 0.980 on val families but 0.4467 (below chance 0.50) on template-disjoint test families. This is a within-study, same-metric, same-layer comparison — nothing about published-vs-replicated scale differs between the val and test numbers — yet the score collapses. That rules out a cross-study scale/metric mismatch as the driver of the OUT_OF_BAND gender result: the collapse is explained by generalization failure across template families, not by scale incomparability. The education PERM_ANOMALY (permuted-label accuracy 0.546/0.410 vs chance 0.333) is likewise a within-run permutation-test artifact, not a published-vs-replica scale issue."
          },
          {
            "confound": "Noise floor: the worry that reported test accuracies (both the IN_BAND ones and the anomalous ones) merely reflect chance-level performance dressed up by a small, high-variance test set (n_test 150-244), rather than genuine learned probe signal.",
            "status": "RULED_OUT",
            "crux": "The protocol's own permutation-null baseline per task is the direct noise-floor instrument, and every task's outcome diverges from its permutation null for a documented non-noise reason: age (test 0.9467 vs perm 0.189/0.197) and SES (test 1.0000 vs perm 0.383/0.202) clear the noise floor by a huge margin, ruling out chance-level performance as the explanation for their IN_BAND status; gender's failure is not noise either — its permutation baseline sits near chance (0.493/0.527) but the actual test accuracy of 0.4467 falls *below* that, while val accuracy at the identical layer was 0.980, so the mechanism is documented template/distribution-shift overfitting (full confusion in the rows), not floor noise; education's anomaly is the inverse of noise floor — its permuted-label accuracy (0.546/0.410) is itself far above chance (0.333), meaning even scrambled labels are separable, i.e. leakage/artifact structure, not indistinguishable-from-noise performance. In no task does the data look like signal-free noise."
          },
          {
            "confound": "Ceiling/floor effects could make the reported band matches uninformative: a task too easy could push any correlated signal to ceiling (masking whether TalkTuner's actual mechanism is being reproduced), or too hard could flatten scores at a floor regardless of probe quality.",
            "status": "RULED_OUT",
            "crux": "Whether anchor_ses's ceiling accuracy (1.0000) reflects a genuine, label-dependent signal or a trivial/leaky shortcut that would saturate regardless of true labels — the permutation control distinguishes these and shows the former."
          },
          {
            "confound": "single-case/family overfit: with only 12 total cue families split 7 train/2 val/3 test, a probe can memorize surface patterns in the tiny held-out family set rather than learning a generalizing demographic direction, inflating (or, on failure, catastrophically collapsing) reported test accuracy.",
            "status": "EXPLAINS",
            "crux": "Gender's val accuracy 0.980 (same layer 6) collapsed to test 0.4467 (below 0.50 chance) on the template-disjoint test families, proving that this pipeline's family-disjoint split does NOT prevent overfit/non-generalization; no per-family breakdown exists to show ses's ceiling (test=1.0000, only 3 held-out families, n=183) or age's 0.9467 are immune to the same mechanism."
          }
        ],
        "rejected_findings": [],
        "run_queue": [],
        "author_queue": [
          "an alternate readout probe for anchor_gender that averages/mean-pools residuals over the full appended message (or over the last-k tokens) instead of the single last token, retrained at the same recorded layer on the identical train/val/test split, to test whether accuracy on the disjoint test families recovers — only the single fixed last-token readout is on record."
        ],
        "runner_results": null
      },
      {
        "id": "probe-expertise-level",
